e language, in written or spoken documents or presentations.", "type": "secondary_7"}, {"content": "Understands important and some specific information and aspects of attitude and intention using a range of appropriate language, in written or spoken documents or presentations.", "type": "secondary_8"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "The Future", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "We have four ways of talking about the future.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "When you talk about things that are not decided or when you make a decision at the moment of speaking for the future, you use the simple future <span class=\"italic\">will</span>. <br> <span class=\"strong\">Example:</span> <span class=\"italic\">I'll serve a meal where I work.</span> <br><br>  Intentions and plans use <span class=\"italic\">be going to</span>. <br> <span class=\"strong\">Example:</span> <span class=\"italic\">I'm going to use it every day!</span> <br><br>  For things that are arranged, or booked for the future, you use the <span class=\"italic\">Present Continuous</span> tense. <br> <span class=\"strong\">Example:</span> <span class=\"italic\">The web designer is helping you out for a year</span>. <br><br>  To talk about schedules or timetables in the future, you use the <span class=\"italic\">Simple Present</span> tense. <br> <span class=\"strong\">Examples:</span> <span class=\"italic\">Your website. It comes online tomorrow</span>. <br><br>", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Thanking People and Responding to Thanks", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You can thank people to show appreciation for a gift or an act of kindness  and respond to thanks in great variety of ways. Here  are some examples:", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "<span class=\"strong\">Vanessa</span>: Oh Sally! What can I say! It's amazing!</br> <span class=\"strong\">Sally</span>: Glad you like it.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":2,\"columns\"